      "title": "Family · grandparents, uncles & cousins",
      "items": [
        {
          "en": "grandad / grandfather",
          "pl": "dziadek",
          "icon": "👴",
          "gender": "m"
        },
        {
          "en": "grandma / grandmother",
          "pl": "babcia",
          "icon": "👵",
          "gender": "f",
          "explain": "babcia and ciocia are the warm everyday words, the ones a family actually uses. The formal babka and ciotka exist but sound cold said to someone's face."
        },
        {
          "en": "uncle",
          "pl": "wujek",
          "gender": "m"
        },
        {
          "en": "aunt",
          "pl": "ciocia",
          "gender": "f"
        },
        {
          "en": "cousin (male)",
          "pl": "kuzyn",
          "gender": "m"
        },
        {
          "en": "cousin (female)",
          "pl": "kuzynka",
          "gender": "f",
          "explain": "Polish builds the female word from the male one with -ka, exactly as it did with nauczyciel to nauczycielka. So kuzyn gives kuzynka."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "family_own",
      "title": "Family · husband, wife & grandchildren",
      "items": [
        {
          "en": "wife",
          "pl": "żona",
          "gender": "f"
        },
        {
          "en": "husband",
          "pl": "mąż",
          "gender": "m",
          "explain": "mąż ends in a consonant, so it is masculine like dom and brat. The tail letter ż does not make it feminine."
        },
        {
          "en": "grandson",
          "pl": "wnuk",
          "gender": "m"
        },
        {
          "en": "granddaughter",
          "pl": "wnuczka",
          "gender": "f",
          "explain": "Same -ka as in kuzynka, but the k of wnuk cannot survive in front of it, so it turns into cz: wnuk, wnuczka."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "family_plural_only",
      "title": "Family · two words that are always plural",
      "items": [
        {
          "en": "parents (always plural)",
          "pl": "rodzice",
          "gender": "pl",
          "explain": "rodzice has no everyday singular. For one of them you simply name them: mama or tata."
        },
        {
          "en": "children (plural of dziecko)",
          "pl": "dzieci",
          "icon": "🧒",
          "gender": "pl",
          "explain": "A neuter noun normally takes -a in the plural (okno gives okna). dziecko refuses and gives dzieci. It is one of a handful of words you learn whole rather than build."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
